<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gita Govinda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Gita</a> Govinda is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> lyrical poem of the 12th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayadeva">Jayadeva</a>, describing the love of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and the cowherd Radha. Its songs became central to Vaishnava devotion in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and are still sung in the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagannath">Jagannath</a> at Puri. The poem also inspired generations of painters and the Odissi dance tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gita-govinda.jpg" alt="Gita Govinda" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Subhashish Panigrahi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Original_palm_leaf_manuscript_of_Gita_Gobinda_(Gita_Govinda)_written_by_Jayadeba_(Jayadeva),_Odisha_state_museum,_Bhubaneswar_2013.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
